{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792934628,"nonce":"R5VnuPcMfvxQdn/hKV0C+q2p198=","title":"Typer accepts calls with too many arguments; Haxe rejects them","message":"class Main {\n      static function t(v:Bool):Void {}\n      static function main() { t(true, 3); trace(\"ran\"); }\n    }\n\nhaxe 4.3.6: `Main.hx:3: characters 36-37 : Too many arguments`. rayzor compiles and runs it, lowering a CallDirect with more arguments than the callee's MIR signature.\n\nThe compiled tiers drop the extra argument silently; the MIR interpreter's decoder rejects it (\"call to `t` passes 3 arguments, it takes 2\"). That is how it surfaced: with hot code compiled in the background, the interpreter now decodes functions the old synchronous tier-up compiled before they ran. TestInt64 and TestReflect called the conformance shim's `t(v)` / `f(v)` with a forwarded `pos`, as upstream unit.Test's `t(v, ?pos)` allows; the shim now matches upstream.\n\nThere is no call-arity check in the typer at all (only macro_probe.rs has the message). A fix has to handle rest arguments, optional and default parameters, extern overloads, Dynamic and function-typed callees, and constructors, and will surface any call the suite currently gets away with.","files":null}]}